A shared `applyDiscount` helper looked reasonable when both callers matched:

```java
double applyDiscount(double price, double pct) {
    return price * (1 - pct);
}
```

Then checkout needed a minimum-order threshold, and the loyalty program needed to cap the discount amount — two unrelated rules the original callers never had:

```java
double applyDiscount(double price, double pct,
                      boolean hasMinOrder, double minOrder,
                      boolean hasCap, double capAmount) {
    if (hasMinOrder && price < minOrder) return price;
    double discounted = price * (1 - pct);
    if (hasCap) return Math.max(discounted, price - capAmount);
    return discounted;
}
```

The function now serves two callers' diverging needs through flags neither caller fully uses — the fix is splitting back into `applyCheckoutDiscount` and `applyLoyaltyDiscount`, each with its own real rule.
